put('HYPOTHESES-AND-FALSE-POSITIVES.md',f'''# Previous hypotheses and false-positive checks

## A — Anonymous loopback authority access

Final independent disposition: **{auth['verdict'] if auth else 'not retained'}**. Empty-token middleware and fixed service-actor routes permit the demonstrated synthetic bound-authority read/cancel. Configured token rejects the same unauthenticated request; operator diagnostics requires a configured credential independently. Present foreign WebSocket Origins are rejected.

The route fixture explicitly skipped lifespan and bound disposable authority state. The stock current opener supplies no ledger/enrollment witness or legacy authorization; mandatory startup fails closed without an admitted embedding. A different-UID reachable serving deployment and browser exploit were not established. Missing incoming Host checks and CORS behaviour alone are insufficient proof. Read the complete final record and exact blockers in NEEDS-VALIDATION.md/findings.json. Evidence: agents/hunt-http/artifacts/auth_route_probe.txt and the7-case current regression artifact.

## B — Filesystem ancestor replacement

Application helpers use absolute paths, symlink checks and leaf no-follow/type/UID/mode/link controls. They do not pin every ancestor with descriptor-relative operations or validate each ancestor's ownership/write permissions. A different OS principal must first have relevant ancestor replacement authority. Same-UID process/key control is trusted by the documented contract.

The minimum deterministic fixture swapped a same-UID ancestor only at the final writable SQLite connection and restored the expected pathname before subsequent fences. SQLite rejected construction with `OperationalError: attempt to write a readonly database`; no fabricated task was accepted. This exact chain was repeated against the later current capture with the same result. See agents/hunt-ledger/artifacts/ancestor_connection.txt and agents/parent-current/artifacts/ancestor-current.txt. This disproves that tested chain; it does not prove every possible ancestor race is impossible.

Repository-ancestor metadata was observed without opening state or credentials: `/` and `/opt` were root-owned755; `/opt/apps` was UID1000-owned755; the workspace/projects/JARVIS ancestors were UID1000-owned750, without group/world write mode bits. Actual configured checkpoint/archive/custody ancestors, ACLs, mount/sticky controls and a rename-capable different UID were not inspected. No cross-UID qualification was performed. The broader deployed claim remains unqualified and has no severity.

Current broker successor securefs uses directory-relative no-follow traversal and owner/write-mode checks on every component; its positive fixture evidence cannot be transplanted to the application's absolute-path helpers. Suggested application hardening: require trusted non-writable ancestors, retain verified directory descriptors for supported leaf operations and authenticate/fence the actual SQLite connection before takeover. SQLite's path/WAL semantics require deliberate handling; a generic file no-follow flag is not an ancestor guarantee.

## Other rejected escalations

- Synthetic executor readiness does not describe legacy chat-tool authority. Real adapter registration is blocked, while legacy local tools have their own safety/dry-run/confirmation path.
- Actual duplicate note append was not expanded into arbitrary shell replay; Level2 confirmation remains in place.
- Unrelated same-UID process termination was not labelled OS privilege escalation.
- Per-session WebSocket exceptions and a finite VAD cap failure were not labelled demonstrated shared-service outages.
- Generic Electron IPC forwarding has only hide/show main-process handlers; no privileged arbitrary operation was established.
- First-capture incomplete Sprint5 method/proposal-field integration observations were resolved in the reviewed later captures and are not current findings.
- Historical security reports, old suite totals, examples and synthetic keys are not evidence of current vulnerabilities or production credentials.
''')
